export const body = "<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p>Inverse kinematics is usually known as the technology that connects game characters' feet to the ground. Whenever you see characters dynamically position themselves on uneven terrains, it is<strong> Inverse Kinematics </strong>in action. So, what is it actually?</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/3057ccf26689d7715d4e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/3057ccf26689d7715d4e.webp\" width=\"1117\" height=\"416\" style=\"--source-width:274px\" alt=\"Illustration 1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/e56388034fbb6bd197df.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/e56388034fbb6bd197df.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"651\" style=\"--source-width:274px\" alt=\"Illustration 2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/9c2a2e7dbaa78f51e07f.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/9c2a2e7dbaa78f51e07f.webp\" width=\"633\" height=\"351\" style=\"--source-width:293px\" alt=\"Illustration 3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p>Before we talk about Inverse Kinematics (IK), we should have a brief look at Forward kinematics (FK).</p>\n<p>FK animates an action from the parent bones, meaning that you can and have to control each children bone for a full motion.</p>\n<p>IK is the exact opposite of that: you take control of a children bone and the parent bones will move accordingly. This means when a character lands his feet on a surface, you don’t have to move the thighs or bend the knees manually to adjust the pose.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>The 2004 title <em>Shadow of the Colossus</em> used this technique to a great extent. In this game, you play as Wander, and your objective is to take down 13 giant creatures called Colossi. Of course, it is no easy task to climb on these gargantuan beasts, as they will move and shake you off their bodies. And when they do that, Wander's body will be influenced, shifting to different states of flailing.</p>\n<p>Animating every one of these states is a huge undertaking, and it may end up looking too authored and unrealistic. So the development team used IK (in conjunction with other systems) to animate this kind of body movement. Basically, they turned Wander's hands into an anchor point on which his body sway accordingly.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/946b5f366394ce9ea756.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/946b5f366394ce9ea756.webp\" width=\"494\" height=\"351\" style=\"--source-width:274px\" alt=\"Illustration 4\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/62ec9f5a29eb97fcc975.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/62ec9f5a29eb97fcc975.webp\" width=\"562\" height=\"359\" style=\"--source-width:293px\" alt=\"Illustration 5\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p>Here is an interesting application from <em>God of War (2018)</em>. Director Cory Balrog shared that in real life, when you punch someone, your fist will stay on the contact point momentarily while your body keeps moving. This realistic phenomenon makes the hit feel weighty and powerful.</p>\n<p>To replicate that effect in the game, the team at Sony Santa Monica used IK on the Leviathan axe. When it hits an enemy, it will stick to their body momentarily while the momentum carries on on the rest of Kratos's body.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Thanks to IK, animators are able to save tons of time and effort authoring motion for characters. It is a flexible tool that keeps seeing more advanced implementations by different developers. And it is fascinating when we realize all those advances started with a footstep.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/6970d99722dd359019f3.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/6970d99722dd359019f3.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"860\" style=\"--source-width:274px\" alt=\"Illustration 6\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div>";
